+### `sol`, `wallet`, `chain`, `ipfs` (permissions `wallet`, `chain`, `ipfs`; this fork)
+
+The badge's own wallet: one keypair in NVS that apps never see, transactions built and
+signed on the badge, and Solana's JSON-RPC and IPFS uploads straight from it, without the
+badge server. NVS is plain flash, so keep only what you'd be fine losing in it. The
+console sets it up:
+
+```
+wallet new [force] | import <secret> | export yes | forget yes
+wallet rpc [<https url> | devnet | mainnet]      -- chain.rpc's RPC; devnet by default
+wallet ipfs [key <token> | url <url> | off]      -- ipfs.add's key and endpoint (Filebase by default)
+wallet mint [import <secret> | off]              -- a mint keypair for the next launch
+```
+
+`sol` needs no permission. Addresses are base58 strings; seeds, instruction data and
+messages are binary strings (`string.pack` builds them). Numbers past 2^31 are decimal
+strings.
+
+```lua
+sol.encode(bytes)                 -- base58, up to 64 bytes
+sol.decode(address [, n])         -- its n bytes (default 32), or nil
+sol.pda({seed, ...}, program)     -- address, bump; up to 16 seeds of up to 32 bytes
+sol.ata(owner, mint, token_program)
+sol.u64(n)                        -- 8 bytes little-endian; n an integer or a decimal string
+local msg = sol.message{
+  payer = address,
+  blockhash = base58,
+  instructions = {{program = address, accounts = {{address, signer, writable}, ...}, data = bytes}, ...},
+  budget = {cu_limit = 200000, priority_fee = 5000, loaded_data = bytes},   -- optional
+}
+```
+
+`sol.message` compiles the keys as Solana does (payer first, then signers and writable
+accounts) and returns the message, binary: a legacy one (up to 1232 bytes), or with
+`budget` a v1 one (SIMD-0385, up to 4096 bytes) that carries its compute budget itself:
+`cu_limit` (required), `priority_fee` in lamports for the whole transaction, and
+`loaded_data` (default 64 MB). Up to 6 instructions, 24 accounts and 400 data bytes each.
+
+```lua
+wallet.address()                  -- base58, or nil with no wallet
+wallet.keypair()                  -- address of a new throwaway keypair (a mint), gone when the app stops
+wallet.mint()                     -- address of the console's `wallet mint import`, or nil
+wallet.mint_used(address)         -- erase that imported mint once it made its coin; true if it was it
+wallet.sign(msg, prompt, function(ok, r) end)   -- r = {signature = base58, tx = base64} or why not
+```
+
+`wallet.sign` asks the owner in the OS's own dialog, showing the app, the cluster, `prompt`
+(up to 100 characters) and the fee payer; the script can't answer it. It signs with the
+wallet and this run's keypairs (`wallet.keypair`, `wallet.mint`, up to 4), whichever the
+message names as signers, and hands back the wire transaction to send.
+
+```lua
+chain.cluster()                   -- "devnet" | "mainnet" | "testnet" | "custom", from the RPC URL
+chain.rpc(method, params, function(ok, result) end)
+chain.rpc(method, params, {url = "https://..."}, function(ok, result) end)   -- another endpoint
+ipfs.ready()                      -- true when an upload key is set
+ipfs.add("images/coin.png", function(ok, cid) end)                   -- a bundled file, up to 2 MB
+ipfs.add({name = "metadata.json", data = text}, function(ok, cid) end)   -- up to 4 KB of text
+```
+
+`chain.rpc` posts the JSON-RPC call to the RPC set with `wallet rpc`, or to `url` (https,
+up to 200 characters: a Jito block engine, say), over Wi-Fi. `result` is the call's
+`result`, decoded, with integers past 32 bits as strings; an RPC error comes back as
+`ok` false and `"rpc <code>: <message>"`. Answers are up to 8 KB: ask for what fits
+(`dataSlice`, `limit`). `ipfs.add` uploads with the console's key, which Lua never sees.
+
